# Commits to skip when running git blame — pure formatting, no behaviour change.
#
# GitHub's blame view reads this file automatically. Plain `git blame` does not:
# each clone needs it turned on once, which is not something this file can do for you.
#
#     git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs
#
# Only add a commit here if it changed formatting and nothing else. A commit that
# reformatted files *while* changing code does not belong in this list — skipping it
# would hide the real change along with the whitespace. That is why the InspectCode
# commit (d03df6f) is absent: it reformatted 25 files while fixing real findings.
#
# Worth knowing what this can and cannot do. It re-attributes lines the ignored commit
# *modified*, by looking them up in the parent commit. It cannot re-attribute a line the
# commit *created* — and a reflow that collapses a four-line call onto one line creates
# lines rather than modifying them. For the commit below that is most of the residual
# blame, so this file changes almost nothing for it in practice. `git blame -w` is what
# actually suppresses reflow noise; on SevenZipExtractor.cs it takes the reformat from 59
# attributed lines down to 2, where this file takes it from 59 to 59.
#
# It is here for the next pure re-indent, where it does work, and to record the policy.

